          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Julian</strong><br>\nJulian, also known as Julian the Apostate, was born in Constantinople in 331 AD to a noble family closely related to the ruling Constantinian dynasty. He was raised by his mother after his father was killed in a purge of the imperial family. Despite the early loss of his father, Julian received an extensive education, which included studies in philosophy and religion, reflecting the diverse intellectual traditions of his time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nJulian's rise to power was unexpected. He was initially kept away from the throne due to the suspicions and potential threats he posed to his cousin, Constantius II. Despite this, he was declared Caesar in 355 AD and sent to Gaul to deal with the invading Germanic tribes. His success in restoring stability and his growing popularity with the army set the stage for his ascent to the rank of Augustus, or senior emperor, later in his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religious Beliefs</strong><br>\nUnlike his predecessors who were Christian, Julian publicly embraced the traditional pagan religions of Rome and was critical of Christianity. His belief in paganism was shaped by his education and the philosophical currents that celebrated Hellenistic traditions. He believed the moral and spiritual health of the empire required a return to its ancient religious practices.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
