      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Emperor Julian's Policies</strong><br>\nAs emperor, Julian attempted to reinvigorate Rome's pagan religion and its practices. He ordered the reopening and restoration of pagan temples and provided them with funding. He also sought to create a hierarchy within the pagan priesthood, similar to that of the Christian church, and he promoted religious tolerance, except towards Christianity, which he viewed with suspicion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Campaigns</strong><br>\nJulian embarked on several military campaigns to protect the empire's territories. His most ambitious campaign was against the Sassanian Empire, Rome's longtime rival in the East. In 363 AD, Julian led a large and well-prepared army into Sassanian territory, aiming to strengthen Rome's eastern frontier and perhaps capture the Sassanian capital, Ctesiphon.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Julian's Persian Expedition</strong><br>\nThe Persian expedition initially met with success as Julian's forces won several engagements and arrived at the walls of Ctesiphon. However, lacking sufficient forces to siege the city and facing a difficult supply situation, Julian was forced to retreat. This retreat turned perilous as the Persians employed a scorched-earth strategy, harassing Roman forces along their withdrawal.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
