          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nJulian's retreat from Ctesiphon ended in tragedy when he was mortally wounded in battle. His death was controversial, with some accounts suggesting he was killed by a Christian who was part of his own forces. Julian died on June 26, 363 AD, after ruling for just 20 months. He was the last non-Christian ruler of the Roman Empire, and his death marked the end of the possibility of paganism regaining its former prominence in the empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Religion</strong><br>\nAfter Julian's death, the empire swiftly returned to Christianity, and his religious reforms were quickly reversed. He is often remembered for his attempts to restore paganism, earning him the title \"the Apostate\" by his Christian contemporaries. However, his policy of religious tolerance did have a lasting impact on how the Roman Empire dealt with different religions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Julian in Historical Memory</strong><br>\nJulian's reign is a subject of significant historical debate. Some view him as a philosopher-king who strove to bring about a moral and cultural renaissance; others see him as a failed ruler whose policies were out of touch with the predominantly Christian empire he ruled. Regardless, Julian remains a notable figure for his unique approach to the religious and political challenges of his time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
